  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Disability equity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">A local OPD, Lavame&apos;a Ta&apos;e&apos;iloa Disabled People Association INC (LATA) received core funding and TA support to enhance organisational capacity, continue advocacy around the Convention on the Rights of People with Disability (CRPD) as well as to provide information and training including on the 4 outer islands of Tonga to 40 participants.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender equality</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia provided funding to local Women&apos;s Rights Organisation, the Women and Children&apos;s Crisis Centre (WCCC), Tonga Women in ICT (TWICT) and Ma&apos;a Fafine moe Famili (MFF) through the Tonga Australia Support Platform (TASP). The grants include core funding for operations as well as programs and activities.  The WCCC is Tonga&apos;s only functioning crisis centre providing counselling, legal assistance, first response, child protection and safe housing for victims and survivors of domestic violence. WCCC&apos;s core operations and programmes are fully funded by Australia. TWICT works to increase more women in the STEM space and encouraging a network of Tongan women with expertise in ICT. MFF provides critical services for the elderly and people with disabilities. The organisation also advocates for gender equality at grassroots and community level.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported the Tongan Government on two significant public financial management reforms. First, through the DFAT funded PFM Adviser to the Ministry of Finance, the Ministry has established a PFM Reform Technical Committee consisting of key government CEOs. The Committee is actively having oversight of the PFM reform roadmap. Secondly, through DFAT technical assistance of the PFM adviser and a short term legal adviser, the Ministry submitted to Cabinet a PFM bill that is now awaiting parliament deliberations. Key elements of the bill touch on critical reforms, replacing outdated legislation and further expanding the principles of fiscal responsibility, transparency and accountability.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Civil society organisations (CSOs) in Tonga face systemic challenges&#x2014;limited core funding, high staff turnover, and weak internal systems&#x2014;hindering service quality, especially in remote areas. In 2024-2025, the Australian Government, through the Tonga Australia Support Platform (TASP), provided core funding and tailored technical support to eight CSOs. TASP conducted systems assessments and delivered capacity-building in financial management, safeguarding, disability inclusion, and monitoring and evaluation. National workshops fostered peer learning and collaboration. CSOs strengthened governance, secured sustainable funding, expanded outreach, and delivered inclusive services. Across all partners, over 8,900 people were reached in 2024&#x2013; 2025&#x2014;79% women, 5% LGBTQI+, and 5% persons with disabilities. Women&#x2019;s Economic Empowerment Programs were restored and CSOs were able to secure government contracts, retain staff and expand services. As one leading CSO Director shared, this partnership &#x201c;helped us to set our house in order, and once our house is in order we can attract others to support us also.&#x201d;</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender equality</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">DFAT, through the Tonga Australia Support Platform (TASP) provides significant assistance to the operations and program activities of Tonga&apos;s Women and Children&apos;s Crisis Centre (WCCC). In this reporting period, 1,033 incidence of services were provided by WCCC through Australian funding support. &#x200d; 236 incidences of victim-survivors accessing crisis centres (74% are women) &#x200d; - 70 incidences of victim-survivors accessing helpline (100% women) &#x200d; - 557 incidences of victim-survivors accessing face-face counselling support (94% are women, 9% PWD and 69% children) &#x200d; - 125 incidences of victim-survivors from rural &amp; remote areas accessing online counselling (70% are women) &#x200d; - 45 incidences of victim-survivors accessing safe housing support (89% are women) .</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
